x\\in [-5,0].$ Notice that $x=-2$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/2f46cbe001623cacc7e7ae8f54d73f2bbc9ce685a9f75ffa93d78a9c621722fe.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,-1)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{-2-3}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{-5}{4}=-1.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.25.$" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247221 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247222 13194139533691 true] [17592186247221 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533691 true] [17592186247221 1041 "field-1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247221 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247222 13194139533691 true] [17592186247221 1110 #uuid "c088649a-aa65-5524-a912-daf84f1fff2a" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247222 1034 "-\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247222 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247222 1111 #uuid "26f105f4-b3bf-5664-915d-200f8ba0e8cf" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247223 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247224 13194139533691 true] [17592186247223 1089 "q-344884" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247223 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533691 true] [17592186247223 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/338c4ecb15136af6f7dca3eb9cf01e396ba1df7f64525bebeaa435cb800b618b.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-3g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247223 1113 #uuid "17d145fa-837e-54b2-bb60-66f02cc2b4e3" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247223 1138 false 13194139533691 true] [17592186247223 1145 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-3{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=2{f}^{′}(-2)-3{g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-3,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-3,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/97303b0f041d638b25388c322828f59bb335a8bf20a7641a62edcbae87490c64.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-3,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{0-(-3)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-3,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{0-3}{0-(-3)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =2{f}^{′}(-2)-3{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =2(1)-3(-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247225 13194139533691 true] [17592186247224 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533691 true] [17592186247224 1041 "field-1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247224 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247225 13194139533691 true] [17592186247224 1110 #uuid "96960299-7def-5795-8e16-124fd5b18608" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247225 1034 "5" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247225 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247225 1111 #uuid "87a6a45b-33ea-5cde-bcc9-bce3562acd27" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247226 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247227 13194139533691 true] [17592186247226 1089 "q-344897" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247226 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533691 true] [17592186247226 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/7af6b87184781cf0f6a3132f375e7917bf4f4cd8ef8b0a4979ab949ac9d6b6c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247226 1113 #uuid "546fe089-ce4e-5070-9ca7-a3ebf5ffc7c5" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247226 1138 false 13194139533691 true] [17592186247226 1145 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=1$ and $h(2)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-2.$ So, ${h}^{′}(2)=-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247227 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247228 13194139533691 true] [17592186247227 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533691 true] [17592186247227 1041 "field-1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247227 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247228 13194139533691 true] [17592186247227 1110 #uuid "9af6049d-cb0a-5609-b9eb-e16683fccb6b" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247228 1034 "-1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247228 1111 #uuid "b1234ba5-e6ff-5385-b4e0-a053c766480b" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247229 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247230 13194139533691 true] [17592186247229 1089 "q-344901" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247229 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533691 true] [17592186247229 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/2d98be8a4f23dd7c8708fd6b9b5b5956b58ceb33bf5223c6eee5222735ccc10e.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(0)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247229 1113 #uuid "7f7f8e77-23eb-5cbe-9752-86ee91d856d4" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247229 1138 false 13194139533691 true] [17592186247229 1145 "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(0)={f}^{′}(0)\\cdot h(0)+f(0)\\cdot {h}^{′}(0).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(0)=-1$ and $h(0)=-3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(0)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${h}^{′}(0)=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(0) & ={f}^{′}(0)\\cdot h(0)+f(0)\\cdot {h}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247230 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247231 13194139533691 true] [17592186247230 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247232 13194139533691 true] [17592186247230 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533691 true] [17592186247230 1041 "field-1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247230 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247232 13194139533691 true] [17592186247230 1110 #uuid "6c529786-22af-5b7f-8b1c-cf9477b104cd" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247231 1034 "1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247231 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247231 1111 #uuid "94646780-7fbb-5341-b61a-9e35ca85d89f" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247232 1034 "4" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247232 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247232 1111 #uuid "9c81edfb-0495-56a0-9ad4-02612551509a" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247234 13194139533691 true] [17592186247233 1089 "q-344910" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247233 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533691 true] [17592186247233 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/9d7feec44f6c49abea5029cc733540f96b4e0f044aaf55dd1a38cf0f76a51a54.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247233 1113 #uuid "ce0e62d6-6fff-5ccc-bcf7-000ee9c56ffa" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247233 1138 false 13194139533691 true] [17592186247233 1145 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=3$ and $g(2)=-2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $2.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 2}{{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247234 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247235 13194139533691 true] [17592186247234 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533691 true] [17592186247234 1041 "field-1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247234 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247235 13194139533691 true] [17592186247234 1110 #uuid "a229eb40-69ae-558e-b60c-8cfd151ec061" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247235 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247235 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247235 1111 #uuid "764e4958-488b-563b-b56b-f54e9cc36c84" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247236 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247237 13194139533691 true] [17592186247236 1089 "q-48949" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247236 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533691 true] [17592186247236 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/54c2987e0078899132d5a350a87e0e1283bd919e8fbe065b86574c2df2348e2f.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247236 1113 #uuid "177a71d2-beae-5a4b-b74a-ea30696afd5a" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247236 1138 false 13194139533691 true] [17592186247236 1145 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)-f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3)}{(g(3))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(3)=-1$ and $g(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,5)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,\\frac{7}{2})$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(3)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)-f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3)}{{(g(3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-1)\\cdot 1}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247238 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247239 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247240 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247241 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247242 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1041 "selection" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247240 13194139533691 true] [17592186247237 1110 #uuid "03c1cdb5-823d-53a1-b038-a25a6803729e" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247238 1034 "3" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247238 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247238 1111 #uuid "705267c5-fdd9-5720-b4e3-f4f0d84d67a1" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247239 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247239 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247239 1111 #uuid "ea52eb55-2f46-5f56-950e-0f774e0d37aa" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247240 1034 "6" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247240 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247240 1111 #uuid "3d20dc49-2f20-5e3b-ac7c-e56fc8f5ee4c" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247241 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533691 true] [17592186247241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533691 true] [17592186247241 1111 #uuid "6d879e02-b44c-5eb3-b159-a73d7157ba2b" 13